WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.161
Welcome back to CS104.

2
00:00:02.161 --> 00:00:08.643
In Part One, we treated complete statements as single units: p, q,

3
00:00:08.643 --> 00:00:09.724
and r.

4
00:00:09.724 --> 00:00:15.126
Now we open those statements and examine their internal structure.

5
00:00:15.126 --> 00:00:20.528
That shift lets us reason about objects, properties, and quantities.

6
00:00:20.528 --> 00:00:27.010
We will begin with predicates and single quantifiers, learn how to negate

7
00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:33.493
quantified claims precisely, compare statements containing two quantifiers, and finish with quantified

8
00:00:33.493 --> 00:00:34.033
arguments.

9
00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:39.975
Listen especially for the domain and the order of the quantifiers.

10
00:00:39.975 --> 00:00:46.457
Those two details often decide whether a mathematical sentence is true, false,

11
00:00:46.457 --> 00:00:48.078
or even meaningful.

12
00:00:48.078 --> 00:00:50.276
Here is our route.

13
00:00:50.276 --> 00:00:56.871
First, we turn expressions with variables into statements by attaching universal or

14
00:00:56.871 --> 00:00:57.970
existential quantifiers.

15
00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:04.564
Next, we study universal conditional statements, their negations, and the familiar contrapositive,

16
00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:06.213
converse, and inverse.

17
00:01:06.213 --> 00:01:12.258
Then we move to two variables, where quantifier order becomes crucial.

18
00:01:12.258 --> 00:01:18.852
Finally, we use rules of inference and diagrams to test quantified arguments.

19
00:01:18.852 --> 00:01:25.447
The ideas build in sequence: a quantified argument is only as clear

20
00:01:25.447 --> 00:01:28.744
as the quantified statements inside it.

21
00:01:28.744 --> 00:01:35.338
Keep asking three questions: what is the domain, which variables are bound,

22
00:01:35.338 --> 00:01:40.284
and what evidence would establish or refute the claim?

23
00:01:40.284 --> 00:01:45.126
Section two point one introduces predicates and quantified statements.

24
00:01:45.126 --> 00:01:51.583
A predicate is not yet a complete assertion because it contains one

25
00:01:51.583 --> 00:01:53.197
or more variables.

26
00:01:53.197 --> 00:01:58.578
A quantifier tells us how broadly the predicate must hold.

27
00:01:58.578 --> 00:02:03.958
Universal claims say the predicate holds for every allowed value.

28
00:02:03.958 --> 00:02:08.801
Existential claims say at least one allowed value works.

29
00:02:08.801 --> 00:02:14.181
This distinction is the foundation for definitions, specifications, and proofs.

30
00:02:14.181 --> 00:02:20.638
When a theorem begins with “for every integer” or a program requirement

31
00:02:20.638 --> 00:02:26.557
says “there exists an input,” it is using exactly this language.

32
00:02:26.557 --> 00:02:29.732
A predicate is a sentence containing variables.

33
00:02:29.732 --> 00:02:35.176
Before substitution, it is an open sentence rather than a true-or-false statement.

34
00:02:35.176 --> 00:02:38.805
Consider two x plus five y equals seven.

35
00:02:38.805 --> 00:02:44.249
If x is one and y is one, the equation becomes seven

36
00:02:44.249 --> 00:02:46.517
equals seven, which is true.

37
00:02:46.517 --> 00:02:51.961
If x is two and y is four, it becomes twenty-four equals

38
00:02:51.961 --> 00:02:53.776
seven, which is false.

39
00:02:53.776 --> 00:02:58.312
The predicate itself did not change; the substituted values did.

40
00:02:58.312 --> 00:03:03.756
This is why we write a predicate as a function-like expression such

41
00:03:03.756 --> 00:03:06.478
as p of x and y.

42
00:03:06.478 --> 00:03:11.922
It records a rule that produces a truth value once suitable values

43
00:03:11.922 --> 00:03:12.829
are supplied.

44
00:03:12.829 --> 00:03:17.250
We name predicates with letters and display their variables explicitly.

45
00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:22.555
Let p of n mean “n is an odd number.” Then p

46
00:03:22.555 --> 00:03:26.976
of two is a complete statement, and it is false.

47
00:03:26.976 --> 00:03:32.281
P of three is also a complete statement, and it is true.

48
00:03:32.281 --> 00:03:37.586
Showing the variable prevents us from confusing the rule with one particular

49
00:03:37.586 --> 00:03:38.028
instance.

50
00:03:38.028 --> 00:03:40.681
It also prepares us to quantify.

51
00:03:40.681 --> 00:03:45.986
We may ask whether p of n is true for every integer

52
00:03:45.986 --> 00:03:51.291
n, or whether it is true for at least one integer n.

53
00:03:51.291 --> 00:03:56.596
Always write down the domain as well: oddness makes sense over integers,

54
00:03:56.596 --> 00:04:01.901
while a different predicate may require real numbers, people, or objects on

55
00:04:01.901 --> 00:04:02.785
a board.

56
00:04:02.785 --> 00:04:07.971
A quantifier states how many values must make a predicate true.

57
00:04:07.971 --> 00:04:13.628
“There are integers x and y such that two x plus five

58
00:04:13.628 --> 00:04:19.286
y equals seven” is existential: it asks us to find a successful

59
00:04:19.286 --> 00:04:19.757
pair.

60
00:04:19.757 --> 00:04:25.415
“For all integers x and y” is universal: it makes a much

61
00:04:25.415 --> 00:04:27.772
stronger claim about every pair.

62
00:04:27.772 --> 00:04:33.429
Attaching a quantifier binds the variables and turns the open predicate into

63
00:04:33.429 --> 00:04:36.258
a statement with a truth value.

64
00:04:36.258 --> 00:04:41.916
Notice that the same equation can appear in both sentences, but the

65
00:04:41.916 --> 00:04:45.216
quantifier changes the burden of proof completely.

66
00:04:45.216 --> 00:04:48.988
For an existential claim, one witness is enough.

67
00:04:48.988 --> 00:04:53.231
For a universal claim, every allowed case must work.

68
00:04:53.231 --> 00:04:59.642
The universal quantifier is written as an upside-down A and read “for

69
00:04:59.642 --> 00:05:06.054
all.” Consider: for every real number x, x squared is greater than

70
00:05:06.054 --> 00:05:06.588
zero.

71
00:05:06.588 --> 00:05:13.000
To make that universal statement true, every real substitution must satisfy the

72
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:13.534
inequality.

73
00:05:13.534 --> 00:05:19.946
But x equals zero gives zero squared greater than zero, which is

74
00:05:19.946 --> 00:05:20.480
false.

75
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.754
One counterexample therefore refutes the entire universal claim.

76
00:05:24.754 --> 00:05:29.029
This example also shows why the domain matters.

77
00:05:29.029 --> 00:05:35.440
The variable is quantified over the real numbers, not over some convenient

78
00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:35.974
subset.

79
00:05:35.974 --> 00:05:42.386
Universal statements demand complete coverage, and a single permitted exception is decisive.

80
00:05:42.386 --> 00:05:47.095
Universal quantifiers are often hidden in ordinary mathematical language.

81
00:05:47.095 --> 00:05:53.374
“The square of every real number is greater than zero” displays the

82
00:05:53.374 --> 00:05:53.898
quantifier.

83
00:05:53.898 --> 00:06:00.177
“The square of a real number is greater than zero” often intends

84
00:06:00.177 --> 00:06:05.932
the same universal reading, even though the word every is absent.

85
00:06:05.932 --> 00:06:12.211
Conditional phrasing can hide it too: “If x is real, then x

86
00:06:12.211 --> 00:06:18.490
squared is greater than zero” normally means this for every x in

87
00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:20.060
the surrounding universe.

88
00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:23.723
When translating, make the hidden quantifier explicit.

89
00:06:23.723 --> 00:06:30.002
Doing so exposes the counterexample x equals zero and prevents a vague

90
00:06:30.002 --> 00:06:33.141
sentence from slipping past careful analysis.

91
00:06:33.141 --> 00:06:38.982
The existential quantifier is written as a backwards E and read “there

92
00:06:38.982 --> 00:06:44.823
exists.” The sentence here claims that there is an integer x whose

93
00:06:44.823 --> 00:06:46.283
square equals two.

94
00:06:46.283 --> 00:06:52.124
To establish an existential statement, we must produce at least one witness

95
00:06:52.124 --> 00:06:54.071
in the stated domain.

96
00:06:54.071 --> 00:06:59.912
No integer works: one squared is one, two squared is four, and

97
00:06:59.912 --> 00:07:03.319
negative integers give the same nonnegative squares.

98
00:07:03.319 --> 00:07:07.700
So this existential statement is false over the integers.

99
00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:13.540
If the domain were real numbers, it would be true because square

100
00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:18.895
root of two and negative square root of two are witnesses.

101
00:07:18.895 --> 00:07:24.249
The predicate stayed the same; the domain changed the truth value.

102
00:07:24.249 --> 00:07:30.464
Existential language also appears in many disguises: “for some integer,” “at least

103
00:07:30.464 --> 00:07:36.679
one integer,” “we can find an integer,” or “the equation has an

104
00:07:36.679 --> 00:07:40.823
integer solution.” Each phrase asks for a witness.

105
00:07:40.823 --> 00:07:47.038
Be careful with the phrase “an integer.” In mathematics, it often means

106
00:07:47.038 --> 00:07:50.146
at least one, not exactly one.

107
00:07:50.146 --> 00:07:56.361
Also remember that proposing a candidate is not enough; the candidate must

108
00:07:56.361 --> 00:08:00.505
belong to the domain and satisfy the predicate.

109
00:08:00.505 --> 00:08:06.720
A real number such as square root of two cannot witness a

110
00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.792
claim quantified over integers.

111
00:08:08.792 --> 00:08:15.007
A good solution states the candidate, verifies domain membership, and substitutes it

112
00:08:15.007 --> 00:08:16.561
into the predicate.

113
00:08:16.561 --> 00:08:21.978
Predicates can be connected by implication or biconditional inside a universal statement.

114
00:08:21.978 --> 00:08:27.396
“For every real x, if x is greater than two, then x

115
00:08:27.396 --> 00:08:32.813
squared is greater than four” has form: for all x, p of

116
00:08:32.813 --> 00:08:35.070
x implies q of x.

117
00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:40.487
The universal quantifier says the conditional must hold for every real x.

118
00:08:40.487 --> 00:08:45.904
Likewise, “for every real x, x equals zero if and only if

119
00:08:45.904 --> 00:08:50.418
two x equals zero” compares two predicates in both directions.

120
00:08:50.418 --> 00:08:55.836
In context, authors sometimes omit the quantifier and write only p of

121
00:08:55.836 --> 00:08:58.093
x implies q of x.

122
00:08:58.093 --> 00:09:03.510
Unless the domain is already clear, expand the sentence before reasoning so

123
00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:07.573
you know what every x is allowed to be.

124
00:09:07.573 --> 00:09:10.940
This summary separates the two proof patterns.

125
00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:16.714
To show that there exists an x with p of x, find

126
00:09:16.714 --> 00:09:19.601
one eligible x and verify p.

127
00:09:19.601 --> 00:09:25.374
To show that for every x, p of x, take an arbitrary

128
00:09:25.374 --> 00:09:31.147
x from the domain and justify p without relying on special features.

129
00:09:31.147 --> 00:09:33.072
Refutation reverses those ideas.

130
00:09:33.072 --> 00:09:37.883
A universal statement is false when we find one counterexample.

131
00:09:37.883 --> 00:09:43.657
An existential statement is false only when we rule out every possible

132
00:09:43.657 --> 00:09:44.138
witness.

133
00:09:44.138 --> 00:09:49.911
These are not merely truth-table facts; they tell us what a proof

134
00:09:49.911 --> 00:09:50.873
must do.

135
00:09:50.873 --> 00:09:52.317
Witnesses support existence.

136
00:09:52.317 --> 00:09:53.760
Counterexamples defeat universality.

137
00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:58.191
Let us apply the notation to a small world.

138
00:09:58.191 --> 00:10:03.114
The domain is the collection of objects on the board.

139
00:10:03.114 --> 00:10:09.022
Predicates describe shapes and colours, such as Triangle of x and Blue

140
00:10:09.022 --> 00:10:10.007
of x.

141
00:10:10.007 --> 00:10:15.915
A two-place predicate such as RightOf of x comma y describes a

142
00:10:15.915 --> 00:10:17.884
relationship between two objects.

143
00:10:17.884 --> 00:10:23.792
This finite model makes quantifiers concrete: “for every object” means inspect each

144
00:10:23.792 --> 00:10:29.700
named object, while “there exists an object” means locate at least one

145
00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:30.685
matching item.

146
00:10:30.685 --> 00:10:36.101
Before answering, read the board carefully and keep the domain fixed.

147
00:10:36.101 --> 00:10:42.009
Nothing outside the grid may be used as a witness or counterexample.

148
00:10:42.009 --> 00:10:44.046
Now test the statements.

149
00:10:44.046 --> 00:10:50.157
A universal such as “for every t, if t is a triangle

150
00:10:50.157 --> 00:10:56.268
then t is blue” requires every triangle to be blue; one non-blue

151
00:10:56.268 --> 00:10:57.796
triangle refutes it.

152
00:10:57.796 --> 00:11:03.907
A biconditional requires both directions, so every blue object must also be

153
00:11:03.907 --> 00:11:04.925
a triangle.

154
00:11:04.925 --> 00:11:11.036
For an existential relation, identify a specific object and check both properties.

155
00:11:11.036 --> 00:11:17.147
For example, “there exists a square y with d to the right

156
00:11:17.147 --> 00:11:21.731
of y” needs one square positioned left of d.

157
00:11:21.731 --> 00:11:23.768
Work sentence by sentence.

158
00:11:23.768 --> 00:11:27.333
For universal claims, search strategically for counterexamples.

159
00:11:27.333 --> 00:11:30.897
For existential claims, search strategically for witnesses.

160
00:11:30.897 --> 00:11:37.137
Negating a universal statement changes it into an existential statement with the

161
00:11:37.137 --> 00:11:38.177
predicate negated.

162
00:11:38.177 --> 00:11:44.417
“Not every real x has x squared greater than zero” means “there

163
00:11:44.417 --> 00:11:50.656
exists a real x whose square is not greater than zero.” The

164
00:11:50.656 --> 00:11:54.296
witness x equals zero proves the negation.

165
00:11:54.296 --> 00:12:00.536
In everyday language, the negation of “No politicians are honest” is not

166
00:12:00.536 --> 00:12:06.776
“All politicians are honest.” It is the weaker claim “Some politician is

167
00:12:06.776 --> 00:12:13.016
honest.” A negation needs only the situation that makes the original sentence

168
00:12:13.016 --> 00:12:13.536
false.

169
00:12:13.536 --> 00:12:18.215
Avoid swinging from one extreme universal claim to another.

170
00:12:18.215 --> 00:12:24.316
Negating an existential statement changes it into a universal statement with the

171
00:12:24.316 --> 00:12:25.333
predicate negated.

172
00:12:25.333 --> 00:12:31.433
“There does not exist an integer x with x squared equal to

173
00:12:31.433 --> 00:12:37.534
two” becomes “for every integer x, x squared is not equal to

174
00:12:37.534 --> 00:12:43.634
two.” Similarly, the negation of “Some computer hackers are over forty” is

175
00:12:43.634 --> 00:12:49.734
“Every computer hacker is forty or under.” Saying merely “some hackers are

176
00:12:49.734 --> 00:12:54.818
forty or under” would not rule out the original witness.

177
00:12:54.818 --> 00:13:00.919
The reliable rule is: move the negation through the quantifier, switch exists

178
00:13:00.919 --> 00:13:04.477
with for all, and negate the predicate.

179
00:13:04.477 --> 00:13:07.868
A universally quantified conditional deserves special care.

180
00:13:07.868 --> 00:13:13.680
Start with “for every person p, if p is blond then p

181
00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:19.493
has blue eyes.” To make this false, we need a particular person

182
00:13:19.493 --> 00:13:21.915
for whom the implication fails.

183
00:13:21.915 --> 00:13:27.727
An implication fails when its hypothesis is true and its conclusion false.

184
00:13:27.727 --> 00:13:33.540
So the negation is: there exists a blond person who does not

185
00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:34.993
have blue eyes.

186
00:13:34.993 --> 00:13:40.806
The same pattern applies to software: the negation of “every program over

187
00:13:40.806 --> 00:13:46.618
one hundred thousand lines contains a bug” is “there exists a program

188
00:13:46.618 --> 00:13:52.431
over one hundred thousand lines that contains no bug.” Switch the quantifier,

189
00:13:52.431 --> 00:13:56.790
then negate the conditional as p and not q.

190
00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:02.117
Universal conditionals have the same three relatives as ordinary conditionals.

191
00:14:02.117 --> 00:14:08.509
The contrapositive reverses and negates the two predicates, and it is equivalent

192
00:14:08.509 --> 00:14:10.107
to the original.

193
00:14:10.107 --> 00:14:16.499
The converse reverses them without negating, and the inverse negates them without

194
00:14:16.499 --> 00:14:17.032
reversing.

195
00:14:17.032 --> 00:14:23.424
The converse and inverse are equivalent to each other but not generally

196
00:14:23.424 --> 00:14:25.022
to the original.

197
00:14:25.022 --> 00:14:29.816
The universal quantifier does not repair an invalid reversal.

198
00:14:29.816 --> 00:14:36.208
If every square is a rectangle, it follows that every non-rectangle is

199
00:14:36.208 --> 00:14:37.274
a non-square.

200
00:14:37.274 --> 00:14:43.666
It does not follow that every rectangle is a square, nor that

201
00:14:43.666 --> 00:14:46.329
every non-square is a non-rectangle.

202
00:14:46.329 --> 00:14:51.600
Try the displayed example: for every real x, if x is greater

203
00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:55.553
than two, then x squared is greater than four.

204
00:14:55.553 --> 00:15:00.824
The contrapositive says: for every real x, if x squared is not

205
00:15:00.824 --> 00:15:05.216
greater than four, then x is not greater than two.

206
00:15:05.216 --> 00:15:10.487
The converse says: if x squared is greater than four, then x

207
00:15:10.487 --> 00:15:15.758
is greater than two; negative values such as minus three show that

208
00:15:15.758 --> 00:15:17.076
this is false.

209
00:15:17.076 --> 00:15:22.346
The inverse says: if x is not greater than two, then x

210
00:15:22.346 --> 00:15:27.617
squared is not greater than four; minus three refutes that as well.

211
00:15:27.617 --> 00:15:32.888
A good counterexample must satisfy the new hypothesis and falsify the new

212
00:15:32.888 --> 00:15:33.327
conclusion.

213
00:15:33.327 --> 00:15:38.818
Necessary and sufficient conditions are simply ways of describing the direction of

214
00:15:38.818 --> 00:15:40.191
a quantified implication.

215
00:15:40.191 --> 00:15:45.682
If p of x implies s of x for every x, then

216
00:15:45.682 --> 00:15:49.801
p is sufficient for s: meeting p guarantees s.

217
00:15:49.801 --> 00:15:55.292
The same sentence says s is necessary for p: p cannot occur

218
00:15:55.292 --> 00:15:56.207
without s.

219
00:15:56.207 --> 00:16:01.241
The phrase “p only if s” also means p implies s.

220
00:16:01.241 --> 00:16:06.732
Keep the arrow anchored to the guarantee rather than the word order.

221
00:16:06.732 --> 00:16:11.308
A useful paraphrase is: whenever p holds, s must hold.

222
00:16:11.308 --> 00:16:16.799
That tells you immediately which predicate belongs in the hypothesis and which

223
00:16:16.799 --> 00:16:18.629
belongs in the conclusion.

224
00:16:18.629 --> 00:16:25.145
Rewrite the statement about presidential age without the words necessary or sufficient.

225
00:16:25.145 --> 00:16:31.661
“Being at least thirty-five years old is necessary for being President of

226
00:16:31.661 --> 00:16:38.177
the United States” means: for every person x, if x is President

227
00:16:38.177 --> 00:16:44.693
of the United States, then x is at least thirty-five years old.

228
00:16:44.693 --> 00:16:51.209
The presidency predicate is the hypothesis; the age predicate is the required

229
00:16:51.209 --> 00:16:51.752
conclusion.

230
00:16:51.752 --> 00:16:58.268
The reverse statement would claim that every person aged thirty-five or older

231
00:16:58.268 --> 00:17:02.069
is president, which is obviously not intended.

232
00:17:02.069 --> 00:17:08.585
This simple plausibility check is useful after translating only-if and necessary-condition language.

233
00:17:08.585 --> 00:17:14.020
We now enter section two point two: statements with multiple quantifiers.

234
00:17:14.020 --> 00:17:19.949
With two variables, we must bind both variables to obtain a complete

235
00:17:19.949 --> 00:17:20.443
statement.

236
00:17:20.443 --> 00:17:25.879
The types of the quantifiers matter, but so does their order.

237
00:17:25.879 --> 00:17:31.808
Two universal quantifiers may be exchanged, and two existential quantifiers may be

238
00:17:31.808 --> 00:17:33.785
exchanged, without changing meaning.

239
00:17:33.785 --> 00:17:35.761
Mixed quantifiers are different.

240
00:17:35.761 --> 00:17:41.690
“For every y there exists an x” allows x to depend on

241
00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:42.184
y.

242
00:17:42.184 --> 00:17:48.114
“There exists an x for every y” demands one fixed x that

243
00:17:48.114 --> 00:17:50.090
works for all y.

244
00:17:50.090 --> 00:17:54.537
That dependency is the central idea of this section.

245
00:17:54.537 --> 00:17:59.869
Let p of x comma y mean x plus y equals zero

246
00:17:59.869 --> 00:18:01.647
over the real numbers.

247
00:18:01.647 --> 00:18:03.868
Four quantifier patterns are possible.

248
00:18:03.868 --> 00:18:09.201
“For all x and all y” is false because most pairs do

249
00:18:09.201 --> 00:18:10.978
not sum to zero.

250
00:18:10.978 --> 00:18:16.310
“For every y there exists an x” is true: choose x equal

251
00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:17.643
to negative y.

252
00:18:17.643 --> 00:18:22.975
“There exists an x such that for every y” is false: one

253
00:18:22.975 --> 00:18:26.085
fixed x cannot cancel every real y.

254
00:18:26.085 --> 00:18:31.418
“There exist x and y” is true; for example, zero and zero.

255
00:18:31.418 --> 00:18:33.639
The equation did not change.

256
00:18:33.639 --> 00:18:37.194
The quantifier structure changed what counts as success.

257
00:18:37.194 --> 00:18:42.427
A double universal statement claims p of x comma y for every

258
00:18:42.427 --> 00:18:44.607
pair in the two domains.

259
00:18:44.607 --> 00:18:49.840
To prove it, take arbitrary x and arbitrary y and reason generally.

260
00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:53.329
To refute it, one bad pair is enough.

261
00:18:53.329 --> 00:18:58.562
For example, x plus y equals zero is not true for all

262
00:18:58.562 --> 00:19:03.795
real x and y because x equals one and y equals one

263
00:19:03.795 --> 00:19:04.667
gives two.

264
00:19:04.667 --> 00:19:09.900
By contrast, the algebraic identity x squared minus y squared equals open

265
00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:15.133
parenthesis x plus y close parenthesis times open parenthesis x minus y

266
00:19:15.133 --> 00:19:19.494
close parenthesis is true for all real x and y.

267
00:19:19.494 --> 00:19:21.675
An identity survives every substitution.

268
00:19:21.675 --> 00:19:26.561
A double existential statement asks for at least one successful pair.

269
00:19:26.561 --> 00:19:31.003
To prove it, present both witnesses and verify the relation.

270
00:19:31.003 --> 00:19:36.333
For x plus y equals zero, x equals one and y equals

271
00:19:36.333 --> 00:19:37.665
minus one work.

272
00:19:37.665 --> 00:19:42.996
For the claim that there exist integers x and y with x

273
00:19:42.996 --> 00:19:48.326
less than y and x greater than y, no pair can work

274
00:19:48.326 --> 00:19:51.435
because the two inequalities contradict each other.

275
00:19:51.435 --> 00:19:56.766
To refute a double existential, we must show the relation is impossible

276
00:19:56.766 --> 00:19:58.098
for every pair.

277
00:19:58.098 --> 00:20:03.428
That is a much stronger task than failing to find an example

278
00:20:03.428 --> 00:20:03.873
quickly.

279
00:20:03.873 --> 00:20:08.867
In the first mixed pattern, for every y there exists an x

280
00:20:08.867 --> 00:20:11.781
such that p of x comma y.

281
00:20:11.781 --> 00:20:15.526
We may choose a different x for each y.

282
00:20:15.526 --> 00:20:20.521
For x plus y equals zero, choose x equal to negative y.

283
00:20:20.521 --> 00:20:25.515
For y equals x squared over the real numbers, the claim “for

284
00:20:25.515 --> 00:20:30.510
every real y there exists a real x with y equals x

285
00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:35.504
squared” is false because negative y values have no real square root.

286
00:20:35.504 --> 00:20:40.498
To refute this pattern, select one y for which no x can

287
00:20:40.498 --> 00:20:40.915
work.

288
00:20:40.915 --> 00:20:45.909
The outer universal tells us which value to challenge; the inner existential

289
00:20:45.909 --> 00:20:49.239
tells us that every possible response must fail.

290
00:20:49.239 --> 00:20:54.276
The other mixed pattern begins with existence: there exists an x such

291
00:20:54.276 --> 00:20:58.054
that for every y, p of x comma y.

292
00:20:58.054 --> 00:21:01.832
We must choose one fixed x before y varies.

293
00:21:01.832 --> 00:21:06.869
For x plus y equals zero over the reals, no fixed x

294
00:21:06.869 --> 00:21:08.548
works for every y.

295
00:21:08.548 --> 00:21:13.586
For x times y equals zero, x equals zero is a successful

296
00:21:13.586 --> 00:21:17.784
universal witness because zero times every real y is zero.

297
00:21:17.784 --> 00:21:22.821
To prove the statement, name the fixed x and then verify arbitrary

298
00:21:22.821 --> 00:21:23.241
y.

299
00:21:23.241 --> 00:21:28.278
Do not quietly choose a new x after seeing y; that would

300
00:21:28.278 --> 00:21:30.797
prove the previous quantifier order instead.

301
00:21:30.797 --> 00:21:34.131
The everyday example makes the distinction vivid.

302
00:21:34.131 --> 00:21:39.846
“For every boy, there is a girl who loves that boy” permits

303
00:21:39.846 --> 00:21:42.704
a different girl for each boy.

304
00:21:42.704 --> 00:21:48.420
“There is a girl who loves every boy” requires one particular girl

305
00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:50.325
connected to all boys.

306
00:21:50.325 --> 00:21:56.041
The second statement implies the first, because the same girl can serve

307
00:21:56.041 --> 00:21:58.899
as the witness for each boy.

308
00:21:58.899 --> 00:22:02.233
The first does not imply the second.

309
00:22:02.233 --> 00:22:07.949
This is a dependency question: may the existential witness depend on the

310
00:22:07.949 --> 00:22:09.377
universally chosen value?

311
00:22:09.377 --> 00:22:15.093
Read quantifiers left to right and imagine a game in which values

312
00:22:15.093 --> 00:22:17.475
are selected in that order.

313
00:22:17.475 --> 00:22:20.355
This first summary covers matching quantifiers.

314
00:22:20.355 --> 00:22:26.115
To show a double universal is true, check arbitrary x and arbitrary

315
00:22:26.115 --> 00:22:26.595
y.

316
00:22:26.595 --> 00:22:30.435
To show it false, find one counterexample pair.

317
00:22:30.435 --> 00:22:35.715
To show a double existential is true, find one witness pair.

318
00:22:35.715 --> 00:22:39.555
To show it false, rule out every pair.

319
00:22:39.555 --> 00:22:42.915
The proof and refutation patterns are dual.

320
00:22:42.915 --> 00:22:48.675
Universal claims are hard to prove but easy to refute; existential claims

321
00:22:48.675 --> 00:22:54.435
are easy to prove once a witness is found but harder to

322
00:22:54.435 --> 00:22:54.915
refute.

323
00:22:54.915 --> 00:23:00.675
Let the quantifier tell you what evidence to seek before you begin

324
00:23:00.675 --> 00:23:01.635
manipulating formulas.

325
00:23:01.635 --> 00:23:04.056
Now summarize the mixed cases.

326
00:23:04.056 --> 00:23:09.866
To prove “for every y there exists an x,” start with arbitrary

327
00:23:09.866 --> 00:23:13.256
y and construct a possibly y-dependent x.

328
00:23:13.256 --> 00:23:18.582
To refute it, find one y for which every x fails.

329
00:23:18.582 --> 00:23:24.392
To prove “there exists an x for every y,” present one fixed

330
00:23:24.392 --> 00:23:28.265
x and show it works for arbitrary y.

331
00:23:28.265 --> 00:23:34.075
To refute it, show that every proposed x has at least one

332
00:23:34.075 --> 00:23:35.043
defeating y.

333
00:23:35.043 --> 00:23:37.464
Notice the alternating response pattern.

334
00:23:37.464 --> 00:23:43.274
Negation will formalize exactly these proof games by switching each quantifier in

335
00:23:43.274 --> 00:23:43.759
place.

336
00:23:43.759 --> 00:23:49.238
To negate multiple quantifiers, move the negation inward one quantifier at a

337
00:23:49.238 --> 00:23:49.695
time.

338
00:23:49.695 --> 00:23:55.174
Each universal becomes existential, each existential becomes universal, and the order stays

339
00:23:55.174 --> 00:23:56.087
the same.

340
00:23:56.087 --> 00:24:01.567
The negation of “for every y there exists an x with p”

341
00:24:01.567 --> 00:24:07.046
is “there exists a y such that for every x, not p.”

342
00:24:07.046 --> 00:24:11.156
That says one y defeats all possible x choices.

343
00:24:11.156 --> 00:24:16.635
The negation of “there exists an x for every y with p”

344
00:24:16.635 --> 00:24:22.115
is “for every x there exists a y with not p.” That

345
00:24:22.115 --> 00:24:27.138
says every proposed fixed x can be defeated by some y.

346
00:24:27.138 --> 00:24:31.247
These forms match the refutation strategies we just described.

347
00:24:31.247 --> 00:24:34.158
Negate the displayed integer statement carefully.

348
00:24:34.158 --> 00:24:39.978
The original says: for every integers x and y, if x plus

349
00:24:39.978 --> 00:24:45.799
y equals zero, then there exists an integer z such that z

350
00:24:45.799 --> 00:24:49.195
squared equals x squared minus y squared.

351
00:24:49.195 --> 00:24:55.015
Negation begins by changing the outer universal pair into an existential pair.

352
00:24:55.015 --> 00:25:00.836
Then negate the conditional: keep x plus y equals zero true, and

353
00:25:00.836 --> 00:25:02.291
negate the conclusion.

354
00:25:02.291 --> 00:25:08.112
Negating the inner existence gives “for every integer z, z squared is

355
00:25:08.112 --> 00:25:13.933
not equal to x squared minus y squared.” The complete negation therefore

356
00:25:13.933 --> 00:25:19.754
asserts a particular pair x and y satisfying the hypothesis for which

357
00:25:19.754 --> 00:25:22.664
no integer z satisfies the equation.

358
00:25:22.664 --> 00:25:27.657
Section two point three turns quantified statements into arguments.

359
00:25:27.657 --> 00:25:34.315
We extend familiar propositional rules by instantiating universal claims at particular objects

360
00:25:34.315 --> 00:25:38.199
and, when justified, generalizing from arbitrary objects.

361
00:25:38.199 --> 00:25:43.748
We will also use set-style diagrams to search for countermodels.

362
00:25:43.748 --> 00:25:50.406
The definition of validity has not changed: an argument is invalid if

363
00:25:50.406 --> 00:25:57.064
there is a possible situation in which every premise is true and

364
00:25:57.064 --> 00:25:59.283
the conclusion is false.

365
00:25:59.283 --> 00:26:05.941
Diagrams are useful because they let us construct or rule out such

366
00:26:05.941 --> 00:26:09.825
situations without listing a large truth table.

367
00:26:09.825 --> 00:26:15.513
Universal direct implication combines a universal conditional with a fact about one

368
00:26:15.513 --> 00:26:15.987
object.

369
00:26:15.987 --> 00:26:21.674
From “for every x, if p of x then q of x”

370
00:26:21.674 --> 00:26:26.414
and p of a, we may conclude q of a.

371
00:26:26.414 --> 00:26:31.628
The ordinary direct-implication pattern appears after we instantiate x with a.

372
00:26:31.628 --> 00:26:35.420
For example, all even integers have even squares.

373
00:26:35.420 --> 00:26:40.634
If a is an even integer, then a squared is even.

374
00:26:40.634 --> 00:26:46.322
The universal premise licenses the substitution, and the second premise activates the

375
00:26:46.322 --> 00:26:46.796
conditional.

376
00:26:46.796 --> 00:26:52.484
Check both: the object must be in the quantified domain, and it

377
00:26:52.484 --> 00:26:54.380
must satisfy the hypothesis.

378
00:26:54.380 --> 00:26:56.522
Universal contrapositive implication works similarly.

379
00:26:56.522 --> 00:27:01.661
From “for every x, p of x implies q of x” and

380
00:27:01.661 --> 00:27:05.516
not q of a, conclude not p of a.

381
00:27:05.516 --> 00:27:10.656
If every human being is mortal and Zeus is not mortal, then

382
00:27:10.656 --> 00:27:12.369
Zeus is not human.

383
00:27:12.369 --> 00:27:17.080
The argument form is valid even if its subject is fictional.

384
00:27:17.080 --> 00:27:22.220
What matters is that the same particular object a appears in the

385
00:27:22.220 --> 00:27:23.933
second premise and conclusion.

386
00:27:23.933 --> 00:27:26.931
This rule is not the inverse error.

387
00:27:26.931 --> 00:27:32.071
We deny the conclusion q and infer denial of the hypothesis p;

388
00:27:32.071 --> 00:27:36.354
we do not deny p and infer denial of q.

389
00:27:36.354 --> 00:27:41.224
Universal transitivity chains two quantified conditionals over a common domain.

390
00:27:41.224 --> 00:27:47.069
If every p-object is a q-object, and every q-object is an r-object,

391
00:27:47.069 --> 00:27:49.991
then every p-object is an r-object.

392
00:27:49.991 --> 00:27:55.835
For an arbitrary x, p of x gives q of x by

393
00:27:55.835 --> 00:28:01.680
the first premise, and q of x gives r of x by

394
00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:02.654
the second.

395
00:28:02.654 --> 00:28:06.550
Because x was arbitrary, the conclusion holds universally.

396
00:28:06.550 --> 00:28:09.472
Watch for mismatched domains or variables.

397
00:28:09.472 --> 00:28:15.317
A chain is only legitimate when the intermediate predicate refers to the

398
00:28:15.317 --> 00:28:17.752
same object under compatible quantification.

399
00:28:17.752 --> 00:28:22.128
A diagram can represent a universal categorical statement.

400
00:28:22.128 --> 00:28:28.692
“All integers are rational numbers” means the set of integers lies entirely

401
00:28:28.692 --> 00:28:31.974
inside the set of rational numbers.

402
00:28:31.974 --> 00:28:38.537
Any point inside the integer circle is automatically inside the rational circle.

403
00:28:38.537 --> 00:28:45.101
This spatial containment mirrors universal implication: integer of n implies rational of

404
00:28:45.101 --> 00:28:45.648
n.

405
00:28:45.648 --> 00:28:52.212
Diagrams do not replace definitions, but they make valid moves visible and

406
00:28:52.212 --> 00:28:54.947
help us search for counterexamples.

407
00:28:54.947 --> 00:29:00.417
When testing an argument, draw only what the premises force.

408
00:29:00.417 --> 00:29:05.339
Do not add relationships merely because they seem plausible.

409
00:29:05.339 --> 00:29:11.167
Consider the argument: all human beings are mortal; Felix is mortal; therefore

410
00:29:11.167 --> 00:29:13.595
Felix is a human being.

411
00:29:13.595 --> 00:29:18.937
The first premise places the human set inside the mortal set.

412
00:29:18.937 --> 00:29:23.308
The second places Felix somewhere in the mortal set.

413
00:29:23.308 --> 00:29:28.164
But it does not force Felix into the human subset.

414
00:29:28.164 --> 00:29:33.992
To test validity, we try to satisfy the premises while falsifying the

415
00:29:33.992 --> 00:29:34.478
conclusion.

416
00:29:34.478 --> 00:29:39.820
Place Felix in the mortal region but outside the human region.

417
00:29:39.820 --> 00:29:45.647
If that arrangement is allowed, we have a countermodel and the argument

418
00:29:45.647 --> 00:29:46.618
is invalid.

419
00:29:46.618 --> 00:29:52.446
This is the quantified form of affirming the conclusion, also called the

420
00:29:52.446 --> 00:29:53.417
converse error.

421
00:29:53.417 --> 00:29:56.420
The completed diagram shows the countermodel.

422
00:29:56.420 --> 00:30:02.426
The human circle remains inside the mortal circle, so the universal premise

423
00:30:02.426 --> 00:30:03.427
is true.

424
00:30:03.427 --> 00:30:09.433
Felix is inside the mortal circle, so the particular premise is true.

425
00:30:09.433 --> 00:30:14.437
Felix is outside the human circle, making the conclusion false.

426
00:30:14.437 --> 00:30:20.443
True premises with a false conclusion are possible; therefore the argument is

427
00:30:20.443 --> 00:30:20.944
invalid.

428
00:30:20.944 --> 00:30:26.449
Notice that we are not claiming Felix really is not human.

429
00:30:26.449 --> 00:30:32.455
We are showing that the premises alone do not guarantee that he

430
00:30:32.455 --> 00:30:33.456
is human.

431
00:30:33.456 --> 00:30:39.461
Validity is about what must follow, not what happens to be true

432
00:30:39.461 --> 00:30:41.463
in the intended world.

433
00:30:41.463 --> 00:30:47.986
Now consider: no polynomial functions have horizontal asymptotes; this function has a

434
00:30:47.986 --> 00:30:52.334
horizontal asymptote; therefore this function is not polynomial.

435
00:30:52.334 --> 00:30:55.052
The two categories are disjoint.

436
00:30:55.052 --> 00:31:01.575
The particular function lies in the horizontal-asymptote set, so it cannot lie

437
00:31:01.575 --> 00:31:03.749
in the polynomial set.

438
00:31:03.749 --> 00:31:09.728
No diagram can make both premises true and the conclusion false.

439
00:31:09.728 --> 00:31:12.989
This is a valid contrapositive pattern.

440
00:31:12.989 --> 00:31:19.512
Translating the first premise helps: if a function is polynomial, then it

441
00:31:19.512 --> 00:31:22.773
does not have a horizontal asymptote.

442
00:31:22.773 --> 00:31:29.296
The second premise denies that conclusion, so we deny the polynomial hypothesis.

443
00:31:29.296 --> 00:31:34.677
The converse error has form: every p is q; a is q;

444
00:31:34.677 --> 00:31:36.471
therefore a is p.

445
00:31:36.471 --> 00:31:41.853
The SMU example says all SMU students are smart, Sam is smart,

446
00:31:41.853 --> 00:31:44.543
therefore Sam is an SMU student.

447
00:31:44.543 --> 00:31:49.925
The premises permit many smart people who are not SMU students, so

448
00:31:49.925 --> 00:31:52.167
the conclusion does not follow.

449
00:31:52.167 --> 00:31:57.548
In a diagram, the SMU set is inside the smart set, and

450
00:31:57.548 --> 00:32:01.584
Sam can sit in the smart region outside SMU.

451
00:32:01.584 --> 00:32:06.966
Whenever you see the conclusion reverse the original implication, pause and ask

452
00:32:06.966 --> 00:32:12.347
whether membership in the larger category really forces membership in the smaller

453
00:32:12.347 --> 00:32:12.795
one.

454
00:32:12.795 --> 00:32:17.777
The inverse error has form: every p is q; a is not

455
00:32:17.777 --> 00:32:20.268
p; therefore a is not q.

456
00:32:20.268 --> 00:32:25.249
From all SMU students being smart and Sam not being an SMU

457
00:32:25.249 --> 00:32:29.400
student, it does not follow that Sam is not smart.

458
00:32:29.400 --> 00:32:33.552
Sam may belong to the smart set for another reason.

459
00:32:33.552 --> 00:32:38.533
The valid contrapositive would begin with Sam not being smart and conclude

460
00:32:38.533 --> 00:32:41.024
Sam is not an SMU student.

461
00:32:41.024 --> 00:32:46.006
Keep the two errors distinct: the converse affirms q to infer p,

462
00:32:46.006 --> 00:32:49.742
while the inverse denies p to infer not q.

463
00:32:49.742 --> 00:32:53.063
Neither move is guaranteed by p implies q.

464
00:32:53.063 --> 00:32:56.493
Let us bring the pieces together.

465
00:32:56.493 --> 00:33:01.639
Predicates become statements when variables are assigned or quantified.

466
00:33:01.639 --> 00:33:08.501
Universal claims demand every case and fall to one counterexample; existential claims

467
00:33:08.501 --> 00:33:15.362
need one witness and fail only when every candidate is ruled out.

468
00:33:15.362 --> 00:33:20.508
Negation switches each quantifier and negates the final predicate.

469
00:33:20.508 --> 00:33:27.369
With mixed quantifiers, order controls whether a witness may depend on an

470
00:33:27.369 --> 00:33:28.513
earlier choice.

471
00:33:28.513 --> 00:33:35.374
Quantified inference instantiates universal rules at particular objects, while diagrams expose possible

472
00:33:35.374 --> 00:33:39.376
countermodels and familiar converse or inverse errors.

473
00:33:39.376 --> 00:33:43.379
Complete the Week Two mastery check now.

474
00:33:43.379 --> 00:33:50.240
When an answer surprises you, identify the domain, read the quantifiers from

475
00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:57.101
left to right, and state exactly what would count as a witness

476
00:33:57.101 --> 00:33:58.245
or counterexample.
